course: dictionary
lesson: 15475
title: Strangulation: a serious word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are serious, and you will find them in news reports, history books and safety notices. Strangulation is one of them. It means squeezing someone's neck so tightly that they cannot breathe.
explain: «The safety leaflet warned parents about the risk of strangulation from long cords.» The word risk tells you it is a danger to avoid. Use the clue words around it: risk, cords and warned all point to something dangerous.
explain: The word has other meanings too. In medicine, it can mean something is squeezed so tightly that blood or air cannot flow through it. Figuratively, «The thick weeds caused the strangulation of the young plants» means the weeds stopped the plants from growing, as if choking them.
fact: Strangulation means squeezing something so tightly that breath or blood cannot pass.
fact: «Strangulation hazard» is a warning phrase: something could squeeze or trap a child's neck.
fact: Cords on blinds, ropes and drawstrings can be dangerous for babies and small children, so keep them out of reach.
fact: Strangulation is an uncountable noun, so we say «the strangulation», not «a strangulation» or «strangulations».
fact: Figuratively, strangulation can mean stopping something from growing or working, as if it were being choked.
exercise: «The safety leaflet warned about the risk of strangulation from loose cords.» What does strangulation mean here? | Losing a cord under the bed | Playing a game with a rope | *Squeezing so tightly that breathing stops | Tying a bow neatly
exercise: «The thick weeds caused the strangulation of the young plants.» What does this mean? | *The weeds stopped the plants from growing | The weeds helped the plants grow taller | The plants were watered too much | The plants were moved to a sunny spot
exercise: Why should cords on blinds be kept out of reach? | *So small children cannot get caught and be at risk of strangulation | So the room gets darker | So the blinds look neater | So the cords can be used for skipping
test: Which phrase is used in safety warnings? | sunny picnic | soft pillow | happy birthday | *strangulation hazard
test: What kind of word is strangulation? | *A noun | A question word | A verb | An adjective
exam: Which is the best meaning of strangulation in «The safety leaflet warned about the risk of strangulation»? | Reading a long book | *Squeezing someone so tightly they cannot breathe | Growing very tall | Being very happy
